---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const meta = [
	{ label: 'Discipline', value: 'Packaging · Brand Work · Print' },
	{ label: 'Year', value: 'Ongoing since 2022' },
	{ label: 'Status', value: 'Active client relationship' },
];

const jumps = [
	{ href: '#context', label: 'The Relationship' },
	{ href: '#packaging', label: 'The Packaging' },
	{ href: '#extras', label: 'Extras' },
	{ href: '#spillover', label: 'Spillover Work' },
	{ href: '#reflection', label: 'Reflection' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
	title="Hey Bright Teeth Whitening — IslandTwig Studios"
	description="Multi-year packaging work for Hey Bright Teeth Whitening, plus spillover work that came through the same relationship."
	path="/design/hey-bright"
	eyebrow="Brand & Packaging · Ongoing Client"
	heading="Hey Bright Teeth Whitening."
	tagline="Multi-year packaging work for my sister Ashley's teeth-whitening business. Three product lines in market, refreshed this year. Plus a piece of spillover work for one of her course attendees."
	meta={meta}
	heroImage="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/hero.jpeg"
	heroAlt="Hey Bright Bright Strips teeth whitening box on a neutral gray background"
	summaryText="Three teeth-whitening packages currently in production. A sweater and a course-attendee map built alongside the line. A piece of separate packaging work for one of Ashley's course clients."
	jumps={jumps}
	ctaHeading="Need packaging that has to live on a shelf?"
	ctaText="I take on packaging and brand work for product-based businesses. Sports identity packages start at $500."
	ctaPillLabel="See services"
>
	<!-- THE RELATIONSHIP -->
	<section class="study-body">
		<div class="study-section" id="context">
			<div class="eyebrow">The Relationship</div>
			<h2>Family rate, professional output.</h2>
			<p>Hey Bright is my sister Ashley's teeth-whitening business. The work has been ongoing for a few years, picking up in the last two as her product line grew. Most of it happens through text messages. She has a need, I send options, she picks one, we iterate. The pace is informal but the output isn't.</p>
			<p>Three current product lines are in production. Each one was updated this year as the brand refined. A few extras have come up along the way that aren't packaging but live in the same brand world. And one piece of paid work landed through her network rather than through her catalog, which is included here because it's part of how this relationship has actually worked.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- THE PACKAGING -->
	<section class="study-body">
		<div class="study-section" id="packaging">
			<div class="eyebrow">The Packaging</div>
			<h2>Three product lines, one visual system.</h2>
			<p>Bright Strips, Glow &amp; Go, and Hey Triple Care all sit on the same shelf and have to read as one brand. The wordmark family carries across the line. Each product gets its own color treatment to differentiate at a glance, but the structural moves stay consistent: a flat front, a tight name lockup, and a back panel that handles the dense information without crowding.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="study-image-block">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/lifestyle-shot.jpeg" alt="Hey Bright Bright Strips package held in hand" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">Bright Strips, the flagship product, in context.</div>
	</div>

	<div class="study-image-block">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/bright-strips-dieline.jpeg" alt="Bright Strips packaging dieline showing front, back, and side panels flat" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">Bright Strips dieline. Front, back, and side panels laid flat for production.</div>
	</div>

	<div class="study-image-block">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/glow-go-dieline.jpeg" alt="Glow and Go packaging dieline" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">Glow &amp; Go dieline. The second product line, refreshed this year.</div>
	</div>

	<div class="study-image-block">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/triple-care-boxes.jpeg" alt="Stack of Hey Triple Care packages" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">Hey Triple Care, in print. The third product completes the current line.</div>
	</div>

	<div class="study-image-block narrow">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/wordmark-exploration.jpeg" alt="Hey Bright wordmark and brand exploration variants" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">A round of wordmark and banner exploration. The friendly typographic tone carries across the line.</div>
	</div>

	<!-- THE EXTRAS -->
	<section class="study-body">
		<div class="study-section" id="extras">
			<div class="eyebrow">The Extras</div>
			<h2>The pieces that aren't packaging.</h2>
			<p>A few projects have come up alongside the product work. None of them are a full brand extension, but each one had a specific job. <strong>The Smile Club sweater</strong> is a personal piece for Ashley to wear at events. <strong>The course participant map</strong> lives on her website and tracks where her course students come from across the country. <strong>The Tooth Gems mark</strong> was an early piece for one of her side ventures, which we started together and is currently on pause.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="image-pair">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/smile-club-hoodie.jpeg" alt="Hey Bright Smile Club hoodie back design" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/smile-club-mark.jpeg" alt="Hey Bright Smile Club mark flat" />
		</div>
	</div>

	<div class="study-image-block">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/course-attendee-map.jpeg" alt="US map of Hey Bright course participants across the country" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">Course Participant map. Lives on the Hey Bright website and grows as new students sign up.</div>
	</div>

	<div class="study-image-block">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/tooth-gems.jpeg" alt="Hey Bright Tooth Gems mark and product treatment" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">Tooth Gems. An early mark for a parallel product line, currently on pause.</div>
	</div>

	<!-- SPILLOVER WORK -->
	<section class="study-body">
		<div class="study-section" id="spillover">
			<div class="eyebrow">Spillover Work</div>
			<h2>Solid Smiles.</h2>
			<p>Ashley's whitening course includes a packaging component, and at some point she started selling my design work as part of what her students could buy through her. <strong>Solid Smiles</strong> was the first of those projects. The client took Ashley's course, wanted packaging for a teeth-whitening product of their own, and paid Ashley. Ashley paid me. I designed a box and a set of labels.</p>
			<p>Same vertical as Hey Bright, different brand, different audience. The arrangement is the part worth mentioning. When a client relationship goes deep enough that the client starts reselling the service, the work compounds in a way a one-off project never does.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="study-image-block">
		<div class="study-image">
			<img src="/images/case-studies/hey-bright/solid-smiles.jpeg" alt="Solid Smiles teeth whitening box and product labels" />
		</div>
		<div class="study-image-caption">Solid Smiles. Box and label set. Commissioned by Ashley on behalf of one of her course students.</div>
	</div>

	<!-- REFLECTION -->
	<section class="study-body">
		<div class="study-section" id="reflection">
			<div class="eyebrow">Reflection</div>
			<h2>The slow client is the durable client.</h2>
			<p>Hey Bright has produced more shipped packaging than any of the big-arc projects I've worked on. It hasn't done it through a master plan. It's done it by being available when she needs an iteration, fast when she needs a turnaround, and willing to keep the format informal when the work doesn't need a contract for every revision.</p>
			<p>Most freelance portfolios are organized around big stories. The truth is that <strong>a steady client who keeps coming back is a more accurate signal of how a working designer actually delivers value</strong> than any one dramatic project. That's the case Hey Bright makes for itself.</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</CaseStudy>
